Here's a sample C++ code that can organize the ranking of teams based on their total scores using cumulative score calculation methodology:
```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <map>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
    // Define the scores of the teams
    map<string, int> scores = {
        {"Team 1", 30},
        {"Team 2", 40},
        {"Team 3", 50},
        {"Team 4", 20}
    };
    // Calculate the total scores of each team
    map<string, int> total_scores;
    for (auto& score : scores) {
        string team_name = score.first;
        int team_score = score.second;
        total_scores[team_name] += team_score;
    }
    // Sort the teams based on their total scores
    vector<pair<string, int>> sorted_scores(total_scores.begin(), total_scores.end());
    sort(sorted_scores.begin(), sorted_scores.end(),
        [](const pair<string, int>& a, const pair<string, int>& b) {
            return a.second > b.second;
        });
    // Print the ranking of the teams
    cout << "Rank\tTeam\tTotal Score" << endl;
    int rank = 1;
    for (auto& sorted_score : sorted_scores) {
        string team_name = sorted_score.first;
        int total_score = sorted_score.second;
        cout << rank++ << "\t" << team_name << "\t" << total_score << endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```
The code first defines the scores of the teams using a `map` data structure. It then calculates the total scores of each team by iterating through the `scores` map and adding up the scores of each team. The total scores are stored in another `map` called `total_scores`.
The code then sorts the teams based on their total scores using a lambda function as the sorting criteria. The sorted teams are stored in a `vector` of pairs called `sorted_scores`.
Finally, the code prints the ranking of the teams by iterating through the `sorted_scores` vector and printing the rank, team name, and total score of each team. The rank is incremented after each team is printed.

