To design a data structure for the to-do list, we can use a tree-like structure where each node represents a task, and its children represent sub-tasks. Each node will have the following properties:
- Task ID: A unique identifier for the task.
- Task Name: A string that represents the name of the task.
- Description: A string that describes the task in detail.
- Priority: An integer that represents the priority of the task.
- Due Date: A date that represents the due date of the task.
- Reminder: A date that represents the reminder for the task.
- Status: A flag that represents the status of the task (e.g., incomplete, complete, in progress).
- Sub-tasks: A list of child nodes that represent sub-tasks.
To handle multiple users, we can create a separate tree for each user and store them in a map where the key is the user ID, and the value is the corresponding tree.
To track progress and completion of tasks, we can add a completion flag to each node that represents whether the task is complete or not. We can also add a progress counter to each node that represents the number of completed sub-tasks.
To provide analytics on individual and team productivity, we can traverse the tree and count the number of completed tasks, incomplete tasks, and in-progress tasks for each user. We can also calculate the completion percentage and average completion time for each task.
To handle concurrent access and updates from multiple users, we can use locking and synchronization mechanisms such as synchronized blocks or locks. We can also use optimistic locking to prevent conflicts between concurrent updates.
Here's an example Java code for the data structure:
```
public class Task {
    private int taskId;
    private String taskName;
    private String description;
    private int priority;
    private Date dueDate;
    private Date reminder;
    private boolean completed;
    private int progress;
    private List<Task> subTasks;
    // getters and setters
}
public class UserTasks {
    private int userId;
    private Map<Integer, Task> tasks;
    // getters and setters
}
public class ToDoList {
    private Map<Integer, UserTasks> userTasks;
