The result of the equation (6 + 4) x 11 is:
\begin{align*}
(6 + 4) \times 11 &= 10 \times 11 \\
&= 110
\end{align*}
To express this result in scientific notation with 5 significant figures, we need to move the decimal point 2 places to the left and add a power of 10:
\begin{align*}
110 &= 1.10 \times 10^2
\end{align*}
Therefore, the result of the equation is $1.10 \times 10^2$.
Real-world applications of this equation in the field of physics include calculating the force exerted by an object with a mass of 6 kg moving at a velocity of 4 m/s and experiencing an acceleration of 11 m/s^2. This can be done using the formula:
\begin{align*}
F &= m \times a \\
&= 6 \times 11 \\
&= 66 \text{ N}
\end{align*}
where $F$ is the force in newtons, $m$ is the mass in kilograms, and $a$ is the acceleration in meters per second squared. In this case, the force exerted by the object is 66 N. The equation (6 + 4) x 11 can be used to calculate the total work done by this force over a certain distance, which is an important concept in physics.

